# Legends: guide_legend

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`guide_legend()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/guide_legend.html), in the order the
page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly
cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a
list of what remains.

`guide_legend()` draws a legend: for each of a scale's breaks, a key drawn as the layer's geom draws
it, beside a label. It is chosen in a scale's `guide` argument or in `guides()`, and the legends of
several scales are merged into one where they can be. Its options set the title, the number of rows
or columns, the order of the keys and the aesthetics they are drawn with, and, through a theme of
its own, where the title and labels go and how they look. Giggly has `Giggly.Plot.guide_legend/1`,
chosen in a scale's `guide:` or in `Giggly.Plot.guides/2`, with its `reverse:` option alone, and
reads `alpha`, and a discrete `colour` or `fill`, back with a legend by default, merging those with
one title and the same labels. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with
`Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells
write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`.

## The basic form

`expand.grid()` makes a row for every pair of `X1` and `X2` from 1 to 10, and `value` is their
product. A continuous fill is read back by a colour bar by default; `guide_legend()` gives it a
key for each break instead.

```r
df <- expand.grid(X1 = 1:10, X2 = 1:10)
df$value <- df$X1 * df$X2

p1 <- ggplot(df, aes(X1, X2)) + geom_tile(aes(fill = value))
p2 <- p1 + geom_point(aes(size = value))

# Basic form
p1 + scale_fill_continuous(guide = guide_legend())
```

Not in Giggly yet: tiles (`geom_tile()`).

## Styling the title and labels

A guide's `theme` styles only that guide: here the title to the left of the keys, a large red
italic title, and labels to the left of the keys, aligned right. In the first plot the theme is
`guide_legend()`'s second argument, given without its name.

```r
# Control styles

# title position
p1 + guides(fill = guide_legend(
  title = "LEFT", theme(legend.title.position = "left")
))

# title text styles via element_text
p1 + guides(fill = guide_legend(theme = theme(
  legend.title = element_text(size = 15, face = "italic", colour = "red")
)))

# label position
p1 + guides(fill = guide_legend(theme = theme(
  legend.text.position = "left",
  legend.text = element_text(hjust = 1)
)))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_tile()`, `guide_legend()`'s `title` and `theme` options, and changing the
theme.

## A horizontal legend

```r
# label styles
p1 +
  scale_fill_continuous(
    breaks = c(5, 10, 15),
    labels = paste("long", c(5, 10, 15)),
    guide = guide_legend(theme = theme(
      legend.direction = "horizontal",
      legend.title.position = "top",
      legend.text.position = "bottom",
      legend.text = element_text(hjust = 0.5, vjust = 1, angle = 90)
    ))
  )
```

The keys run across, with the title above them and each label below its key, turned on its
side.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_tile()`, `guide_legend()`'s `theme` option, and changing the theme.

## Keys drawn opaque

```r
# Set aesthetic of legend key
# very low alpha value make it difficult to see legend key
p3 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(vs, am, colour = factor(cyl))) +
  geom_jitter(alpha = 1/5, width = 0.01, height = 0.01)
p3
# override.aes overwrites the alpha
p3 + guides(colour = guide_legend(override.aes = list(alpha = 1)))
```

Keys are drawn as the layer draws its points, here almost transparent; `override.aes` draws them
with an `alpha` of their own.

Not in Giggly yet: jittered points (`geom_jitter()`) and `guide_legend()`'s `override.aes` option.

## Rows and columns of keys

```r
# multiple row/col legends
df <- data.frame(x = 1:20, y = 1:20, color = letters[1:20])
p <- ggplot(df, aes(x, y)) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = color))
p + guides(col = guide_legend(nrow = 8))
p + guides(col = guide_legend(ncol = 8))
p + guides(col = guide_legend(nrow = 8, theme = theme(legend.byrow = TRUE)))
```

`legend.byrow = TRUE` fills the keys in row by row, rather than column by column.

Not in Giggly yet: `guide_legend()`'s `nrow`, `ncol` and `theme` options, and changing the theme.

## Keys in reverse order

```r
# reversed order legend
p + guides(col = guide_legend(reverse = TRUE))
```

`p` is the example above's plot, twenty points coloured by the first twenty letters, R's
`letters[1:20]`, which the cell makes too. R's `col` is short for `colour`. The legend's keys run
from `t` down to `a`.

```elixir
df = %{
  "x" => Enum.to_list(1..20),
  "y" => Enum.to_list(1..20),
  "color" => ~w(a b c d e f g h i j k l m n o p q r s t)
}

p =
  df
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :x, y: :y})
  |> Plot.geom_point(%{colour: :color})

p
|> Plot.guides(colour: Plot.guide_legend(reverse: true))
|> render.()
```
